const faq = [
  { q: 'Do I need a public adjuster if I already have an insurance broker?', a: 'For a large loss, often yes. Your broker placed the policy and manages the account. A public adjuster is licensed to represent you on the claim itself: measuring the loss, building the claim and negotiating it. Your broker can stay informed throughout.' },
  { q: "How is a public adjuster different from the insurance company's adjuster?", a: 'Their adjuster works for the insurance company. A public adjuster is licensed to work only for you, the policyholder. We start from their estimate and measure what it left out.' },
  { q: 'How are public adjusters paid?', a: "No upfront cost. We're paid a percentage of what we recover. In California, our fee can never leave you with less than your insurer had already paid before you hired us. [Public adjuster fees, explained](/fees/)" },
  { q: 'Does hiring a public adjuster slow the claim down?', a: 'It can. A 2010 Florida legislative study of Citizens Property Insurance claims found that claims with a public adjuster took longer to close, and paid more before fees. A loss with art, wine and custom work takes time to document properly. You decide on every offer, including when to settle.' },
  { q: 'When should I call a public adjuster?', a: 'Before you accept an offer, and ideally before the carrier\'s scope is final. Our ability to affect your outcome diminishes with time. If you already have an offer, we can usually reopen scope before you accept.' },
];
